#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5865455-213b-5f5e-9366-420907a81259" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inequality Represented by a Number Line"}] :question/id #uuid "b8a1258e-e69c-528c-be89-3ed5510cdf38" :question/math-academy-id "e-13029" :question/problem "What inequality corresponds to the red colored graph shown in the number line below?\n\n![](images/39/399512ad3596d2d055849d631cfbe45859a74de5e5604fb0da55aadc62a421c9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The red arrow indicates that $x$ should consist of all the values greater than $2.$ In addition, the filled red circle at $2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $2.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents the numbers that are greater than or equal to $2.$ This corresponds the inequality $x \\geq 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21897f76-0c26-5314-a029-3089042bad26" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Number Line for a Given Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "878239be-ff00-592c-85b0-7aa12d232749" :question/math-academy-id "e-13030" :question/problem "Represent $x < -1$ on a number line." :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$<$\" means \"less than.\"  So, the inequality $x < -1$ represents the numbers that are less than $-1.$\n\nOn a number line, the numbers less than $-1$ are the numbers that are left of  $-1.$ So, we should draw an arrow going to the left of $-1.$\n\nHowever, the number $-1$ itself is not less than $-1,$ so we should not include it in our representation. To exclude $-1,$ we draw an empty circle around $-1.$\n\n![](images/57/57499770d310c5f0912e62ec160accd878a1c169c22ce894a48102a39769d209.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5865455-213b-5f5e-9366-420907a81259" :knowledge-point/title "Finding 